An example of offline cryptography is the offline encryption of files on a USB.
If a user wants their offline files to be more secure, they can encrypt the files. This will protect the files from anyone who doesn't have the key to decrypt them.
The most common encryption technique is AES (Advanced Encryption Standard). For this technique, the user would have to first create a 16 byte initialisation vector.
This vector doesn't have to be secret, but does have to be exactly 16 bytes. Next, the user must come up with a secret key. This key must be 128, 192 or 256 bits longs
and must remain secret. Once the initialisation vector, file, and key are loaded into the user's preferred offline program, it encrypts the files on the USB. These files
are safe from hackers on the cloud, and if the user gets a virus/ malware after the files encryption, they cannot be accessed unless plugged into the infected computer.
